# This patch adds a mechanism to selectively enable syntax transform in only
# some Ruby scripts.
#
# * When the Ruby functions `eval` or `instance_eval` are called while
#   `mkxp_syntax_transform_next_eval` is nonzero,
#   `mkxp_syntax_transform_next_eval` is immediately set to 0
#   and then the parser object used by that `eval` or `instance_eval` call has
#   syntax transform enabled (if positive) or disabled (if negative). The value
#   of `mkxp_syntax_transform_next_eval` can be changed from C/C++ to specify
#   which top-level `eval` calls need to have syntax transform enabled.
# * ASTs output by parsers on which `mkxp_parser_enable_syntax_transform()` has
#   been called have a flag set that indicates the AST has syntax transform
#   enabled.
# * Instruction sequences derived from ASTs that are flagged as having syntax
#   transform enabled are themselves flagged as having syntax transform enabled.
# * The syntax transform flag propagates to child instruction sequences of
#   instruction sequences.
# * When the Ruby functions `eval` or `instance_eval` are called from an
#   instruction sequence that has been flagged as having syntax transform
#   enabled, `mkxp_parser_enable_syntax_transform()` is called on the parser
#   object used by that `eval` or `instance_eval` call to flag the parser as
#   having syntax transform enabled. This ensures that the syntax transform flag
#   propagates into `eval` and `instance_eval` calls.
# * The other patches may add code that checks if the syntax transform flag is
#   set on the current parser or instruction sequence, depending on what part of
#   the codebase is being modified, in order to ensure that the syntax transform
#   is only enabled on the Ruby scripts where it should be enabled.

